SAT_CHUNK("rw/text-structure/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.5135d718bd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-0","passage":"<p>For decades, ornithologists assumed that the bright yellow throat patch of the male Kessler's warbler evolved because females prefer mates with the most vivid coloring. Biologist Ines Carvalho tested this assumption by pairing females with males whose patches had been subtly brightened or dulled with nontoxic dye. <u>Females showed no preference for either group, but rival males retreated far more quickly from territories held by brightened birds.</u> Carvalho concludes that the patch may function less as an advertisement to mates than as a warning to competitors.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents an experimental result that confirms the assumption described at the beginning of the text.","It describes the method Carvalho used to alter the appearance of the male warblers without harming them or affecting their behavior.","It presents the experimental result that leads Carvalho to propose an alternative to a long-held explanation.","It introduces a second experiment that Carvalho conducted to address a flaw in her first one."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The underlined sentence reports what happened in Carvalho's experiment: females didn't prefer brighter males, but rivals backed away from them. That result is what leads to the final sentence's alternative explanation (a warning to competitors). It doesn't confirm the old assumption; it undermines it. The method is described in the previous sentence, and there's no second experiment."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.d4ee102966","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-1","passage":"<p>Many readers treat the letters of nineteenth-century merchant Abigail Thorne as a straightforward record of her business dealings. Historian Marcus Oyelaran urges caution. Thorne knew that her correspondence would be read aloud to partners and creditors, he notes, and <u>she routinely described failed ventures as \"postponed\" and debts as \"arrangements.\"</u> Oyelaran argues that the letters are valuable less as a ledger of facts than as evidence of how a merchant managed her reputation.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It identifies the specific business failures that Thorne attempted to hide from her partners.","It explains why many readers have treated Thorne's letters as a reliable business record.","It suggests that Thorne's letters contain factual errors that make them useless to historians.","It gives examples that support Oyelaran's view of how the letters should be read."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The underlined sentence gives examples of Thorne's careful wording (\"postponed,\" \"arrangements\"). Those examples back up Oyelaran's point that the letters show reputation management. The close wrong choice about \"factual errors that make them useless\" goes too far: Oyelaran says the letters are still valuable, just for a different reason. The sentence doesn't name specific failures, and it doesn't explain why others trusted the letters."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.34b1bdefb9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-2","passage":"<p>In most orchestras, the concertmaster, the lead first violinist, tunes the ensemble before a performance. At the Lindqvist Chamber Players, that job rotates among all the musicians, including the percussionists. <u>The practice began as a matter of scheduling convenience in the group's early years, when members often arrived late from other jobs.</u> Today, however, the players describe it as central to their identity: an expression of the belief that no single member should stand above the others.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the Lindqvist Chamber Players value equality among their members.","It suggests that the group's rotating tuning practice has caused problems during performances.","It explains the practical origin of a custom that has since taken on a different significance.","It contrasts the Lindqvist Chamber Players' tuning practice with the practice followed by the concertmasters of most orchestras."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The underlined sentence says the rotation started for a practical reason (scheduling). The next sentence (\"Today, however...\") shows it later came to mean something else (equality). The close wrong choice says the sentence explains why the group values equality, but that meaning comes from the final sentence, not the underlined one. The contrast with most orchestras happens in the first two sentences."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.53d557cc96","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-3","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1920s short story. Nell has returned to her childhood home after many years away.</p><p>The kitchen was smaller than she remembered, as kitchens always are. Nell ran a hand along the table's edge, where a groove still marked the spot her brother had carved with a butter knife one bored winter. <u>She had expected the room to feel like a stranger's; instead, it seemed to have been waiting for her, patient and unsurprised.</u> She set down her bag and, without quite deciding to, reached for the kettle.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the memory that Nell associates most strongly with her childhood home.","It establishes that the kitchen has been changed significantly since Nell last saw it, making the room feel unfamiliar to her.","It reveals that Nell's return produces a feeling different from the one she anticipated.","It suggests that Nell regrets having stayed away from her childhood home for so long."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The underlined sentence contrasts what Nell expected (a stranger's room) with what she actually feels (the room was \"waiting for her\"). The \"regret\" choice is tempting, but the text never says she regrets being away. The kitchen seems smaller but not changed, and the carved groove memory comes in the sentence before."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.24f001857f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-4","passage":"<p>Urban planners often assume that adding bike lanes to a street will reduce car traffic on it. A study of 40 streets in Portaverde complicates that picture. <u>On streets where lanes were added without narrowing the roadway for cars, traffic volume barely changed.</u> Only where lanes replaced a car lane did driving decline, and even then, some of the traffic simply shifted to nearby streets.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a finding that challenges a common assumption about bike lanes.","It explains why some of the traffic in Portaverde shifted to nearby streets.","It presents a finding that supports the assumption that bike lanes reduce car traffic.","It describes the method used to choose the 40 streets included in the study."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Planners assume that bike lanes reduce traffic. The underlined finding, that traffic \"barely changed\" on some streets with new lanes, cuts against that assumption. That is the first of the study's complications. The traffic shift to nearby streets is mentioned only in the final sentence, and nothing is said about how streets were selected."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.1043d65a3d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-5","passage":"<p>Nineteenth-century critics largely dismissed the novels of Harriet Voss as sentimental entertainments for young readers. <u>Yet these same critics borrowed freely from her plots, and several of their own acclaimed works echo her scenes almost line for line.</u> Literary scholar Dana Achebe argues that Voss's low reputation reveals more about the critics' assumptions regarding popular fiction than about the quality of her writing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It points out an inconsistency in the critics' treatment of Voss.","It suggests that Voss copied scenes from the works of the critics who dismissed her.","It presents Achebe's primary evidence that Voss's novels were not sentimental.","It provides evidence that Voss's novels were more popular with young readers than with critics."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The critics dismissed Voss, yet they copied her. That inconsistency sets up Achebe's claim that the low opinion of Voss says more about the critics than about her. The choice about Voss copying the critics reverses who borrowed from whom. The sentence isn't about readership, and it doesn't argue that the novels weren't sentimental; Achebe's claim concerns quality and critics' assumptions."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.925007019c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-6","passage":"<p>Octopuses are famously solitary, and researchers long believed they avoid one another except to mate. But at a site off the coast of eastern Australia that divers nicknamed \"Octlantis,\" dozens of gloomy octopuses have been observed living in close quarters, displaying to one another and occasionally evicting neighbors from dens. <u>The site offers abundant food but few safe places to shelter on an otherwise flat seafloor.</u> Some researchers suggest that such conditions may push normally solitary animals into social interactions they would otherwise avoid.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It offers evidence that gloomy octopuses are naturally more social than other octopus species, regardless of where they live.","It describes conditions that may help explain the behavior observed at the site.","It explains why researchers originally concluded that octopuses avoid one another.","It describes the method divers used to locate the site nicknamed \"Octlantis.\""],"answer":"B","explanation":"The underlined sentence describes the site: plenty of food but little shelter. The last sentence says such conditions may explain why these octopuses interact, so the underlined sentence supplies a possible cause for that social behavior. It says nothing about the original belief, other species, or how divers found the site."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.9de02e7438","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-7","passage":"<p>When the Harwell Public Library eliminated late fees in 2019, some board members predicted that books would go unreturned for months. Instead, the proportion of items returned within three weeks of their due date rose slightly. More striking was a change the board had not anticipated: borrowers who had avoided the library for years because of unpaid fines began returning, and card registrations increased by nearly a fifth. Library director Samuel Oduya now describes fines as a policy that \"protected our books from the very people we exist to serve.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It presents a problem faced by a library, describes several possible solutions, and then explains which solution the library chose.","It describes a policy change, reports that a feared consequence did not occur, and then presents an unexpected benefit of the change.","It presents a prediction about a policy, provides evidence that the prediction was accurate, and then offers a director's defense of the policy.","It describes a policy change, explains the reasoning behind it, and then presents criticism of the change from board members."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text first describes eliminating late fees. It then shows the feared outcome (books going unreturned) didn't happen, and finally reports an unexpected benefit (lapsed borrowers came back). The board members' prediction proved wrong, not accurate, and the text never lays out several possible solutions."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.da3b584d78","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-8","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1911 novel. Mrs. Pell is hosting a dinner for her husband's business associates.</p><p>Mrs. Pell had arranged the table with the care of a general positioning troops. The talkative Mr. Aldous sat beside the deaf Mrs. Grange, where his stories could do no harm; the two rival bankers faced each other across a centerpiece of lilies tall enough to spare them the trouble of conversation. <u>When at last the soup was served, she allowed herself a small, private smile, as a commander might upon seeing the enemy advance exactly where she had predicted.</u></p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It shows that Mrs. Pell is relieved that the dinner has begun without any disruptions from the rival bankers.","It introduces a conflict between Mrs. Pell and her guests that will be developed later in the story.","It reveals that Mrs. Pell regards her guests as enemies whom she hopes to defeat.","It extends a comparison by showing Mrs. Pell's satisfaction that her plan is working."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The first sentence compares Mrs. Pell to a general, and the underlined sentence continues that comparison: she smiles \"as a commander might\" because events are going as planned. The choice about treating guests as literal enemies takes the comparison too literally; the military language describes her careful planning, not hostility. \"Relief\" misses her satisfaction at a successful strategy, and no conflict is introduced."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.1768859e85","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-9","passage":"<p>In 1935, a dust storm carried topsoil from the Great Plains all the way to the Atlantic coast, darkening skies over Washington, D.C. Congressional hearings on soil erosion were underway at the time, and legislators who had been skeptical of federal conservation programs watched the sky turn brown through the windows of the Capitol. <u>Within weeks, Congress passed the Soil Conservation Act.</u> Historians still debate how much the storm itself influenced the vote, but the timing was, at the very least, persuasive.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents evidence that proves the dust storm was the reason that Congress changed its position on conservation.","It presents an outcome that the text suggests was probably, though not certainly, influenced by the storm.","It explains why legislators who had watched the storm from the Capitol had been skeptical of federal conservation programs.","It describes the main provision of a law that historians continue to debate."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The underlined sentence reports the law passed soon after the storm. The final sentence says historians \"debate\" how much the storm mattered but call the timing \"persuasive,\" so the link is likely but not proven. The choice with \"proves\" overstates it. The sentence gives no reasons for skepticism and no details of the law."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.2d6b445db5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-10","passage":"<p>Economists have long used the price of a Big Mac in different countries as an informal gauge of whether currencies are overvalued or undervalued. <u>The logic is simple: if the burger costs much more in one country than another after converting currencies, the more expensive country's currency may be overvalued.</u> Critics note, however, that the measure ignores differences in labor costs, rent, and local taste, all of which can affect the burger's price independently of exchange rates.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It summarizes the critics' objections to the Big Mac measure.","It explains why economists prefer the Big Mac measure to more formal methods.","It explains the reasoning behind a measure that the text then qualifies.","It presents an example of a currency that the Big Mac measure shows to be overvalued."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The underlined sentence explains how the Big Mac measure works. The next sentence (\"Critics note, however...\") points out its limits. No specific currency is named, the objections come afterward, and the text doesn't say economists prefer it to formal methods; it calls it \"informal.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.76cb7f9034","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-11","passage":"<p>The ceramic figurines found at the ancient site of Tell Hamra were once assumed to be children's toys because of their small size and simple features. Archaeologist Leyla Farouk noticed that many showed signs of deliberate breakage and had been buried beneath house floors, often near hearths. Toys, she reasoned, are usually discarded when broken, not buried with apparent care. Farouk proposes that the figurines played a role in household rituals, perhaps meant to protect the home.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To explain how the people of Tell Hamra performed rituals intended to protect their homes","To explain why an archaeologist questions an earlier interpretation of some artifacts","To compare the burial practices at Tell Hamra with those of other ancient settlements","To argue that small ceramic figurines at ancient sites were rarely intended for children"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The text reports the old view (toys), Farouk's observations (deliberate breakage, careful burial), and her new interpretation (ritual objects). That's the question of an earlier interpretation. The choice about figurines \"at ancient sites\" generalizes beyond this one site. The text doesn't describe how the rituals were performed, and it doesn't compare Tell Hamra with other settlements."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.880cfb0b9b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-12","passage":"<p>Honeybee colonies choose new nest sites through a process that resembles a debate. Scout bees that find promising cavities return to the swarm and perform waggle dances, and <u>the vigor of each dance reflects the quality of the site the scout has found.</u> Other scouts inspect the advertised sites and, if impressed, join the dancing. Over hours or days, support for weaker sites fades until the scouts reach a consensus, and the swarm departs for the chosen location.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It identifies a feature of the scouts' behavior that allows better sites to gain support over time.","It explains how scouts determine which of the cavities they find are suitable nest sites for the swarm.","It suggests that the most energetic scouts decide on their own where the colony will build its new nest.","It describes how the swarm travels once a site has been chosen."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Stronger dances for better sites let those sites attract more scouts, which is how the colony reaches a good consensus. The \"most energetic scouts decide\" choice misreads it: dance vigor reflects site quality, and the decision emerges from many scouts. The sentence doesn't say how scouts judge cavities, and travel is only mentioned at the end."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.36b94cfabd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-13","passage":"<p>The architect Mirela Stan is best known for her glass towers, but her early career was spent designing grain silos in rural Romania. <u>Critics rarely mention this work, perhaps because silos seem to belong to engineering rather than architecture.</u> Stan herself insists that the silos taught her everything essential: how to make a massive structure seem light, and how to let function determine form without apology. Her towers, she says, are \"silos with windows.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests why critics have overlooked work that the text presents as important to Stan.","It explains why Stan chose to design grain silos in rural Romania before she began designing glass towers.","It argues that critics have misunderstood the design of Stan's glass towers.","It suggests that Stan's silos were more successful than her glass towers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The underlined sentence gives a possible reason (silos seem like engineering) that critics overlook Stan's silo work. The rest of the text, in Stan's own words, shows that work mattered a great deal. The sentence doesn't discuss the towers' design, why she built silos first, or compare how successful the two kinds of buildings were."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.6581b3981e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-14","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an 1897 essay about travel.</p><p>The traveler who sets out to see everything sees nothing. He moves from cathedral to museum to monument, checking each from a list, and returns home with a trunk full of postcards and a mind as empty as when he left. <u>I would rather spend a week in a single village, learning the name of the baker and the hour the church bells ring, than a month racing across a continent.</u> Such a traveler may see less, but he will have been somewhere.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It concedes that the list-checking traveler gains some knowledge of the places he visits.","It offers the essayist's alternative to the approach criticized earlier.","It describes a village that the essayist visited during a recent journey.","It explains why the essayist was unable to travel across an entire continent."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The essayist first criticizes the traveler who tries to see everything, then offers a preference: stay in one village and know it well. No particular village visit is described, the essayist concedes nothing to the list-checker (\"sees nothing\"), and there's no claim that the essayist couldn't travel."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.848541be12","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-15","passage":"<p>In a 2021 survey, 78 percent of respondents in the city of Lakemont said they supported expanding public transit. Transportation researcher Kofi Mensah cautions against reading too much into that figure. <u>When the same respondents were asked whether they would support a small tax increase to fund the expansion, support dropped to 41 percent.</u> Mensah suggests that broad approval of a policy in principle can mask considerable reluctance to bear its costs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a result that illustrates why Mensah is cautious about the survey's headline figure.","It explains why most respondents opposed expanding public transit in Brantford.","It shows that the survey's questions were worded in a misleading way.","It presents a result that contradicts Mensah's conclusion about public support for policies."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Mensah warns against over-reading the 78 percent figure. The underlined result (support falls to 41 percent once a tax is mentioned) is the reason for that caution and leads directly to his conclusion. It supports him rather than contradicting him. Most respondents supported transit in principle, and nothing suggests misleading wording."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.0cbc1dd738","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-16","passage":"<p>Sea otters are often called a keystone species because of their outsized effect on kelp forests: by eating sea urchins, which graze on kelp, otters allow the forests to flourish. In an estuary in central California, however, ecologist Brent Hughes found that otters also benefit seagrass, a plant that urchins rarely eat. <u>There, otters feed heavily on crabs, which in turn eat the small slugs that clean algae off seagrass blades.</u> With fewer crabs, slug populations grow, and the seagrass thrives.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It provides an example of how otters benefit kelp forests by controlling urchins.","It begins to explain how otters benefit seagrass.","It explains why urchins rarely eat seagrass in the central California estuary.","It suggests that crabs, rather than otters, are the keystone species in the estuary."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Otters eat crabs, crabs eat slugs, and slugs clean seagrass. The underlined sentence starts that chain, and the last sentence finishes it. The kelp-and-urchin example is in the first sentence and works differently. Nothing explains why urchins avoid seagrass, and crabs aren't called a keystone species."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.9b99b11ae0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-17","passage":"<p>Many people believe that the Great Wall of China is visible to the naked eye from the Moon. <u>The claim appeared in print decades before anyone had traveled to space</u>, and astronauts who have looked for the wall from low Earth orbit report that it is extremely difficult to see, since it is narrow and similar in color to the surrounding land. From the Moon, which is roughly a thousand times farther away, it would be invisible.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why astronauts in low Earth orbit have difficulty seeing the wall.","It provides evidence that the wall is more visible from space than many people assume.","It identifies the first astronaut who claimed to have seen the wall from space.","It suggests that a popular belief could not have originated from actual observation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"If the claim was printed before space travel, no one could have seen the wall from space when it began. That undercuts where the belief came from. The astronauts' difficulty is explained later in the sentence (narrowness and color), no astronaut is named, and the text argues the wall is hard to see, not easy."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.81034186b9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-18","passage":"<p>Linguists have documented that speakers of Kuuk Thaayorre, an Aboriginal language of northern Australia, describe locations using cardinal directions rather than terms like \"left\" and \"right\": a speaker might say that a cup is to the southwest of a plate. <u>Such speakers must therefore keep track of their orientation at all times, even indoors.</u> In experiments, Kuuk Thaayorre speakers arranged picture sequences showing the passage of time from east to west, regardless of which way they were facing, whereas English speakers consistently arranged them from left to right.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents the conclusion that the linguists reached after conducting their experiments.","It draws out an implication of a feature of the language that helps explain the experimental results that follow.","It explains why Kuuk Thaayorre speakers avoid using terms like \"left\" and \"right\" when describing where objects are located.","It suggests that English speakers have difficulty keeping track of their orientation indoors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"If you describe everything with compass directions, you must always know which way you're facing. That implication helps explain why Kuuk Thaayorre speakers laid out time from east to west. The sentence doesn't explain why the language lacks \"left\" and \"right,\" it isn't a conclusion drawn after the experiments, and English speakers' sense of direction isn't discussed."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.ef1db1e506","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-19","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1905 short story. Tomas, a young clerk, has been invited to dinner by his employer.</p><p>Tomas had rehearsed his remarks on the walk over: a comment on the weather, a question about Mr. Whitcombe's roses, a modest observation on the price of coal. <u>At the door, however, a maid took his hat before he could decide whether to offer it, and the first sentence Mr. Whitcombe spoke was one that none of Tomas's rehearsals had anticipated.</u> He heard himself answer in a voice he did not recognize.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It shows that Tomas is more comfortable with the household staff than with his employer.","It suggests that Mr. Whitcombe intends to embarrass Tomas in front of the household.","It marks the point at which Tomas's careful preparation begins to prove inadequate.","It reveals that Tomas has misunderstood the purpose of the dinner invitation."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Tomas prepared remarks, but at the door things immediately go off script (\"however\"). He isn't sure about his hat, and his employer says something he didn't expect, so his preparation starts to fail. Nothing shows Whitcombe means to embarrass him or that the invitation's purpose was misunderstood, and Tomas's uncertainty with the maid shows no special comfort with the staff."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.8a868b812d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-20","passage":"<p>The mathematician Emmy Noether proved in 1915 that every symmetry in the laws of physics corresponds to a quantity that is conserved. The fact that physical laws do not change over time, for instance, corresponds to the conservation of energy. <u>Physicists had long known that energy was conserved, but they had treated the principle as a basic observation about nature rather than as a consequence of something deeper.</u> Noether's theorem transformed conservation laws from facts to be memorized into features that could be derived.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that physicists initially rejected Noether's theorem.","It explains how Noether discovered the connection between time and energy.","It clarifies the earlier view of conservation that Noether's work changed.","It provides an additional example of a symmetry that corresponds to a conserved quantity."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The underlined sentence describes how physicists saw conservation before Noether: as a basic observation. The last sentence explains how her theorem changed that. The text doesn't describe how she made her discovery or say physicists rejected it, and the only symmetry example appears in the second sentence."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.eb81798403","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-21","passage":"<p>Some nutrition researchers have argued that the traditional diet of the island of Okinawa explains its residents' remarkable longevity. Epidemiologist Rosa Lindgren is skeptical of explanations that center on any single factor. <u>Okinawans who were born before 1940 also had unusually strong social networks, did physically demanding work into old age, and lived in a region with relatively little air pollution.</u> Any of these conditions, Lindgren notes, could plausibly contribute to long life.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It provides evidence that the traditional Okinawan diet contributes little to residents' longevity.","It identifies factors that support Lindgren's skepticism.","It explains why the traditional Okinawan diet changed after 1940.","It describes the factors that Lindgren has proven to be responsible for Okinawans' long lives."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Lindgren doubts single-factor explanations. The underlined sentence lists other conditions (social networks, physical work, clean air) that could also matter. That supports her skepticism without showing that diet contributes \"little,\" and without claiming she has \"proven\" what causes longevity. Nothing about diet changes after 1940 is mentioned."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.d40d7836ee","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-22","passage":"<p>When the painter Olu Adebayo began exhibiting in the 1970s, galleries in London often hung his canvases in rooms devoted to \"international art,\" apart from British artists. <u>Adebayo had lived in London since the age of four.</u> The curator of a recent retrospective, Helen Marsh, argues that the category said less about Adebayo's work than about the gallery system's assumptions regarding who counted as a British artist.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that Adebayo's early paintings were strongly influenced by British art.","It explains why Adebayo's paintings were often grouped with works by international artists.","It presents a fact that highlights the questionable basis of the galleries' decision.","It provides evidence that Adebayo preferred to exhibit his work outside Britain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Galleries treated Adebayo as \"international\" even though he had lived in London since he was four. That fact exposes the flawed assumption Marsh criticizes. It doesn't justify the grouping; it undercuts it. It also says nothing about his influences or where he preferred to exhibit."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.0ccd80a5b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-23","passage":"<p>Wetlands are often drained to make land available for farming or construction. The benefits of such projects are easy to measure: new acres of cropland, new buildings. The costs are harder to see. Intact wetlands absorb floodwaters, filter pollutants, and store carbon, and a 2020 analysis estimated that the flood protection provided by coastal wetlands in the United States is worth billions of dollars each year. When wetlands disappear, those services do not show up as a loss on any balance sheet, but communities downstream pay for them nonetheless.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It explains why wetlands are drained, describes the history of the practice, and then argues that it should be banned.","It presents an estimate of the value of wetlands, questions the accuracy of that estimate, and then offers an alternative estimate.","It notes a common practice, contrasts its visible benefits with its hidden costs, and then explains who bears those costs.","It describes a problem caused by a common practice, proposes a solution to that problem, and then evaluates the economic costs of the solution."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The text names a practice (draining wetlands), contrasts easy-to-measure benefits with hidden costs, and ends by saying downstream communities pay those costs. It proposes no solution, doesn't question the estimate, doesn't give a history, and doesn't call for a ban."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.7aa66ccb26","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-24","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a 1913 novel. Ada has just received a letter informing her that she has inherited a small house in the country.</p><p>She read the letter twice, then folded it along its creases with great exactness, as though precision might keep its contents from escaping. Her first thought was not of the house, which she had never seen, but of her landlady's face when she gave notice. <u>Ada had rented the same narrow room for eleven years and had never once been late with the rent; she had imagined that she would be carried out of it.</u></p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that Ada's landlady has treated her unfairly over the years.","It reveals that Ada intends to refuse the inheritance and remain in her room.","It shows how settled Ada's life has been.","It explains why Ada has never visited the house she has inherited."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Eleven years in one room, always paying on time, expecting to die there: this shows how fixed Ada's life has been, so the inheritance is a big disruption. Nothing suggests unfair treatment or that she'll refuse the house, and it doesn't explain why she hasn't seen the house."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.04e1c67fce","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-25","passage":"<p>For years, the conventional wisdom in publishing held that short-story collections do not sell. <u>Editors routinely advised writers to hold their stories until they had completed a novel, which could then help sell the collection.</u> The success of several debut collections in the past decade, including two that spent months on best-seller lists, has led some publishers to reconsider, though many remain wary.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It provides evidence that short-story collections sell as well as novels do.","It explains why several debut story collections became best sellers in the past decade.","It describes the reason that some publishers remain wary of short-story collections.","It describes a practice based on a belief that is now being reconsidered."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Because editors believed collections don't sell, they told writers to finish a novel first. The next sentence says that belief is being reconsidered. The sentence doesn't explain the recent best sellers, give sales evidence, or give the reason for the lingering wariness."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.479d614bbe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-26","passage":"<p>Termite mounds in southern Africa maintain remarkably stable internal temperatures despite large swings in the outside air. Engineers once attributed this to a chimney effect: warm air rising and exiting through the top. <u>But when researchers placed sensors inside active mounds, they found that air moved in daily cycles that reversed direction between day and night.</u> The mound, it turns out, works less like a chimney than like a lung, \"breathing\" as the sun heats and cools its outer walls.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents evidence that supports the chimney explanation proposed by engineers.","It introduces the evidence that led researchers to revise an earlier explanation.","It explains how termites build mounds with walls that heat and cool quickly.","It describes the chimney effect that engineers used to explain the mounds' stable temperatures."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"But\" signals a challenge to the chimney explanation. The sensor data showed airflow reversing each day, which led to the new \"lung\" model. The chimney effect is described in the previous sentence, construction isn't discussed, and the evidence undercuts rather than supports the chimney idea."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.b41b723ffa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-27","passage":"<p>The historian Jun Watanabe studies the account books of Edo-period rice merchants. <u>Such records are less dramatic than the diaries of samurai or the decrees of shoguns, and scholars have generally consulted them only for price data.</u> Watanabe reads them differently. By tracking the small loans merchants extended to farmers during poor harvests, he reconstructs a web of obligation and trust that, he argues, held rural communities together more effectively than any official policy.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It summarizes Watanabe's findings about loans between merchants and farmers.","It describes how a type of source has typically been used, setting up a contrast with Watanabe's approach.","It suggests that the price data found in Edo-period account books are unreliable and should be used with caution.","It explains why Watanabe prefers account books to the diaries of samurai."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The underlined sentence explains that scholars usually used account books only for prices. \"Watanabe reads them differently\" sets up his new approach. The text doesn't say why he prefers them, doesn't question the price data, and his findings come afterward."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.8bb53827a1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-28","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1909 poem. The speaker addresses an old oak tree.</p><p>You stood here when the road was mud, / When wagons, not the trains, went by; / <u>You did not ask the road to stay, / Nor mourn it when it turned to rail.</u> / Teach me that patience, ancient friend, / Who watch the world and do not fail.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["They explain why the tree has survived longer than the road.","They express the speaker's regret that the road has been replaced by rail.","They describe the changes that the speaker has witnessed over a lifetime.","They describe a quality of the tree that the speaker then asks to learn."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The underlined lines show the tree accepting change without complaint, and the next line asks, \"Teach me that patience.\" The regret belongs to neither the tree (\"nor mourn it\") nor, explicitly, the speaker. The lines don't explain the tree's survival, and they describe what the tree, not the speaker, has seen."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.c107c83542","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-29","passage":"<p>Many studies have found that people who own dogs live longer on average than people who do not. It is tempting to conclude that dogs improve their owners' health, perhaps by encouraging walks. <u>Yet people who are already healthy and active may simply be more likely to adopt a dog in the first place.</u> To separate these possibilities, researchers have begun following people for years before and after they acquire a pet.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It identifies a flaw in the method used by researchers who follow people for years.","It explains how owning a dog encourages people to exercise more often.","It presents evidence that owning a dog does not affect a person's health.","It raises another possible explanation for a finding."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The underlined sentence suggests the link could run the other way: healthy people get dogs. Long-term studies are designed to sort out which explanation is right. The sentence offers a possibility, not evidence that dogs have no effect. The walking idea comes in the sentence before, and the long-term studies are the proposed fix, not something being criticized."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.d8336ee5ac","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-30","passage":"<p>In the 1860s, the chemist Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the known elements into a table according to their properties. <u>Where no known element fit the pattern, he left gaps, and he predicted the properties of the elements that would one day fill them.</u> When gallium was discovered in 1875 with nearly the density and melting behavior Mendeleev had forecast for one such gap, the table gained a credibility that no mere arrangement of existing data could have earned.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that Mendeleev's table contained errors that were later corrected.","It describes a feature of Mendeleev's work that allowed later discoveries to test it.","It describes the method that chemists used to discover new elements in the 1870s.","It explains how Mendeleev determined the density of gallium before its discovery."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The gaps and predictions made the table testable. When gallium matched a prediction, the table gained credibility. The sentence says Mendeleev predicted properties in general, not that he determined gallium's density. It doesn't mention errors, and it's about Mendeleev's method, not how other chemists found elements."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.7737c14bbe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-31","passage":"<p>The documentary filmmaker Rhea Mistry rarely appears on camera and never narrates her films. Instead, she lets her subjects speak at length, often in single uninterrupted shots lasting several minutes. <u>Some reviewers have described this approach as a kind of absence, as though Mistry had simply switched on a camera and walked away.</u> But the choice of which moments to film, and which to include, is itself an argument, and Mistry's films make theirs with unusual force.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the criticism that eventually led Mistry to change her approach to filmmaking.","It supports the claim that Mistry's films make powerful arguments.","It explains why Mistry chooses not to narrate her films or appear in them on camera.","It presents a characterization of Mistry's style that the text then disputes."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Reviewers call Mistry's style an \"absence.\" The next sentence (\"But...\") argues her choices of what to film and include are a strong presence. The text gives no reason for her not narrating, says nothing of her changing her approach, and the reviewers' view is what the text pushes back against."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.03ac30f503","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-32","passage":"<p>Before the 1990s, most astronomers assumed that planetary systems around other stars would resemble our own, with small rocky planets close to the star and gas giants farther out. <u>The first planet discovered orbiting a sunlike star, however, was a gas giant circling its star in just four days, far closer than Mercury is to the Sun.</u> Since then, astronomers have found so many such \"hot Jupiters\" that some now wonder whether it is our solar system that is unusual.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why gas giants in our solar system are located far from the Sun.","It describes the method astronomers used to discover the first planet orbiting a sunlike star.","It presents a discovery that contradicted an expectation described in the previous sentence.","It shows that hot Jupiters are more common than planetary systems like our own."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Astronomers expected gas giants far from their stars. The underlined discovery was a gas giant very close to its star, which contradicted that expectation. The sentence doesn't explain our solar system's layout or describe methods, and one discovery alone doesn't show that hot Jupiters are more common; that idea comes in the final sentence, and only as something some astronomers \"wonder.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.1c338e9bf2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-33","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a 1916 short story. Lena is watching her younger brother prepare for his first day of work at the mill.</p><p>He had combed his hair three times and still it stood up at the crown. Lena watched him from the doorway and did not tell him it didn't matter. <u>She remembered her own first morning at the mill, and how the foreman had not looked at her hair, or her face, or anything but the clock.</u> She handed him his lunch pail and said only, \"You'll do fine.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reveals that Lena resents her brother for taking a job at the mill.","It shows that Lena was treated more kindly on her first day than her brother will be.","It suggests what Lena knows but chooses not to say.","It explains why Lena's brother is worried about the foreman's opinion of him."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Lena knows the foreman won't care how her brother looks, so his careful combing is pointless and perhaps sad. She keeps that knowledge to herself, saying only \"You'll do fine.\" There's no resentment. Her memory suggests indifference, not kindness, and the text never says her brother is thinking about the foreman."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.79df68c356","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-34","passage":"<p>The Voynich manuscript, a fifteenth-century book written in an unknown script, has resisted every attempt at decipherment. Some scholars have argued that it is a hoax: meaningless symbols arranged to resemble writing. <u>Statistical analyses, however, show that the frequency of its \"words\" follows patterns similar to those found in natural languages.</u> Although this does not prove the text has meaning, it makes the hoax theory harder to sustain, since a forger of that era would have been unlikely to know about such patterns, let alone reproduce them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It proves that the Voynich manuscript was written in a natural language.","It provides evidence that the manuscript was created by a skilled forger.","It explains why earlier attempts to decipher the manuscript's unknown script have failed to reveal its meaning.","It introduces evidence that weakens one theory without settling the question."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The word-frequency patterns make the hoax theory \"harder to sustain,\" but the text explicitly says this \"does not prove the text has meaning.\" So the answer isn't \"proves.\" The sentence doesn't explain failed decipherments, and it cuts against the forger idea rather than supporting it."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.d38bd5bdcc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-35","passage":"<p>The playwright Ama Boateng wrote her first plays for a small theater company that performed in the courtyards of apartment buildings in Accra. <u>Because audiences could wander in and out, she learned to make every scene capable of standing on its own.</u> Critics who later saw her work in conventional theaters sometimes found her plays loosely constructed. Boateng's admirers argue that such critics mistake a deliberate design for a lack of discipline.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that audiences in Accra preferred short scenes to full-length plays.","It explains the origin of a feature that some critics misunderstood.","It explains why Boateng stopped writing plays for courtyard performances.","It presents a criticism of Boateng's plays that her admirers reject."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Wandering audiences taught Boateng to write self-contained scenes. Later critics saw that as loose construction, and her admirers say it's deliberate. The criticism itself comes in the next sentence. The text says nothing about audience preferences or her leaving courtyard theater."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.525f21cfe2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-36","passage":"<p>People often assume that learning a skill such as juggling improves with practice at a steady rate. Data from psychologist Priya Raman's studies suggest otherwise. <u>Participants typically improved rapidly during their first few sessions, then showed little progress for weeks before improving again.</u> Raman argues that these plateaus are not signs of failure but periods in which the brain reorganizes the skill, and she warns that many learners quit just before the second surge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It provides evidence that practice does not improve a person's ability to juggle.","It describes the method Raman used to measure participants' juggling ability.","It explains why many learners quit during the early stages of learning a skill.","It presents a pattern that contradicts a common assumption."],"answer":"D","explanation":"People assume steady improvement. The underlined result shows a burst, a plateau, and another burst, which Raman then explains as reorganization. It doesn't say practice fails (participants did improve), and quitting is mentioned only at the end, as happening before the second surge, not early on. No measurement method is described."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.3d852ac236","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-37","passage":"<p>In 1854, physician John Snow mapped cholera deaths in London's Soho district and found that they clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street. Most doctors at the time believed that cholera spread through foul air. <u>Snow noted that workers at a nearby brewery, who drank beer made with water from a different source, were largely spared.</u> His analysis persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle, though it would be years before the medical establishment accepted that cholera was waterborne.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that beer protected brewery workers from cholera.","It explains why most doctors at the time believed that cholera spread through the foul air of crowded neighborhoods like Soho.","It shows that officials removed the pump handle because of complaints from brewery workers.","It provides an observation that points to water, rather than air, as the source of illness."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The brewery workers breathed the same air but drank different water, and they were largely spared. That points to the water, not the air, and it strengthened Snow's argument. The tempting choice about beer being protective misses the point: what mattered was the different water source. The sentence neither explains the foul-air belief nor involves complaints."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.3.dda8c1e13b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data1-38","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an 1893 essay on reading.</p><p>We are told that the reading of novels wastes time that might be given to history or philosophy. But I have learned more of what men fear from a single chapter of a good novel than from whole volumes of history, which tell me what men did but seldom why. The historian records the battle; the novelist shows me the soldier who could not sleep the night before. Both are necessary. Yet it is the second who teaches me to recognize the first in myself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To argue that novels offer an insight that history rarely provides","To argue that history is a less useful subject of study than philosophy","To describe the essayist's experience reading a particular novel about a soldier the night before a battle","To recommend that readers abandon history in favor of novels"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The essayist responds to the claim that novels waste time. He argues that novels show why people act and what they fear, which history seldom shows. He says \"both are necessary,\" so he isn't telling readers to abandon history. Philosophy is mentioned only in passing, and the soldier is an illustration, not a particular novel."}
]);
